BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 658.0 Td (  **The Myth of the Sacred Spring**) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 648.0 Td (  ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 638.0 Td (  In ancient times, there lived a village divided by a deep ravine. On one side dwelt the Givers) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 628.0 Td (    , blessed with golden seeds. On the other lived the Keepers, who possessed the Sacred Spring) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 618.0 Td (    -magical waters that tripled whatever touched them.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 608.0 Td (  ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 598.0 Td (  The Givers faced a choice each moon: keep their five seeds safe, or cast some across the ravin) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 588.0 Td (    e to the Keepers. The journey was perilous, and many feared their gifts would vanish into da) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 578.0 Td (    rkness.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 568.0 Td (  ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 558.0 Td (  But one day, a Giver named Aster cast three seeds across the chasm in faith. The Keeper caught) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 548.0 Td (     them and placed them in the Sacred Spring. As promised, they multiplied to nine. The Keeper) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 538.0 Td (    , moved by Aster's trust, returned six seeds across the ravine.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 528.0 Td (  ) Tj ET
