BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 568.0 Td (      # The Tale of the Two Wells) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 558.0 Td (      ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 548.0 Td (      In ancient times, two villages were separated by a vast desert. Each village had a single ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 538.0 Td (        well, but a strange magic bound them: any water poured into one well would triple before) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 528.0 Td (         flowing into the other village's well.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 518.0 Td (      ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 508.0 Td (      The villages took turns as the Giver. When the First Village poured water, it flowed three) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 498.0 Td (        fold to the Second Village, who then chose how much to send back through the desert dire) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 488.0 Td (        ctly \(without multiplication\).) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 478.0 Td (      ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 468.0 Td (      At first, fear ruled. The First Village sent little, thinking, "What if they keep it all?") Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 458.0 Td (         Receiving only a trickle, the Second Village returned nothing. Both grew thirsty.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 448.0 Td (      ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 438.0 Td (      Then came the season of drought. The First Village's elder said, "We perish either way. Le) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 428.0 Td (        t us pour generously and trust." They sent their full bucket. The Second Village receive) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 418.0 Td (        d three buckets-abundance! ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 408.0 Td (      ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 398.0 Td (      Grateful and moved by such trust, they sent back two buckets. The First Village now had mo) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 388.0 Td (        re than they started with! The Second Village, having kept one bucket, also thrived.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 378.0 Td (      ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 368.0 Td (      From that day forward, both villages prospered. They learned that the magic was not in the) Tj ET
